.TH "GALAXY\-UPGRADE" "1" "" "galaxy" "Scalar Galaxy Manual"
.SH "NAME"
galaxy\-upgrade \- upgrade galaxy to the latest release
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B galaxy upgrade
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Replaces this galaxy executable with the latest published release, after verifying the download against the checksums published alongside it.
.PP
When galaxy was installed through a package manager (Homebrew or npm) nothing is replaced. That manager owns the installed files, and overwriting them leaves its database describing a file that is no longer there, so the command runs the manager's own upgrade command instead, with its output on this terminal.
.PP
galaxy also checks for a newer release at most once a day and mentions one on standard error. That check is skipped when either standard output or standard error is not a terminal, when CI is set, when running under sudo, and when NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER or GALAXY_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER is set in the environment.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Report the installed and latest versions and exit, without installing anything.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The command completed successfully.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The command failed. The reason is reported on standard error.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command was invoked incorrectly (missing or invalid arguments).
.PP
When galaxy cannot perform the upgrade itself it prints what to run instead and exits 0, having changed nothing: a project dependency, a cache such as npx, an executable it does not own, or a package manager whose own program it cannot locate. A manager it does run exits 1 if it cannot be started or if it fails, and so does a Homebrew upgrade that leaves an older version installed than the latest release. Run galaxy upgrade \-\-check first to learn whether a newer release exists.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
